quired \u0060confidence\u0060 and \u0060confidence_reason\u0060 fields. Based on content analysis: single source, internally consistent, but describes an operational trade-off. Score: \u0060medium\u0060 with reason \u0022One source, internally consistent; describes durability trade-off.\u0022\n\n### Structure Findings\n\n1. **[[orphan-topic]] - orphan page**: The page \u0022Idempotency Keys\u0022 has zero inbound links from any other page or from index.md. It should be linked from index.md\u0027s Technology section since it\u0027s a resilience pattern relevant to retries.\n\n### Review Window (90 days from now)\n\nLooking at timestamps:\n- \u0060stale-topic\u0060 (Sticky Sessions): \u0060last_reviewed: 2025-01-05\u0060, confidence: \u0060low\u0060. This is more than 90 days old (from current date context in the system timestamps which go up to 2026-06), so this is a review candidate.\n\nNow I need to refresh inbound_links and create the report. Let me proceed with the findings report and then address what to fix:\n\n---\n\n## Content Quality\n\n### Contradictory cache invalidation strategies: [[cache-invalidation-ttl]] vs [[cache-invalidation-events]]\n\nThe wiki contains two pages making mutually incompatible claims about cache invalidation. [[cache-invalidation-ttl]] argues that \u0022TTL-based expiry is the only invalidation strategy that gives a hard staleness guarantee, and it should be the default for every cache in this system.\u0022 Meanwhile, [[cache-invalidation-events]] directly contradicts this, stating that \u0022TTL-based expiry is unreliable under bursty writes\u0022 and that event-driven invalidation \u0022should replace TTL-based expiry everywhere in this system.\u0022 These recommendations cannot both be followed in the same system.\n\n**Proposed remediation**: A human should determine which strategy is correct for this system\u0027s use case, or if both are conditionally valid, mark one or both pages as addressing different scenarios (e.g., add context distinguishing \u0022high-volume, bursty writes\u0022 vs. \u0022steady-state reads\u0022). Consider whether one page should be marked \u0060superseded_by\u0060 the other, or if the pages should be reconciled to explain when each strategy applies.\n\n## Metadata Hygiene\n\n### Missing tags on [[undertagged-topic]]\n\nThe page \u0022Read-Through Caching\u0022 (file: \u0060undertagged-topic.md\u0060) has no \u0060tags\u0060 field. It requires at least 2 tags conforming to the Tag Taxonomy (one category prefix \u002B one content-specific tag).\n\n**Proposed remediation**: Add tags: \u0060concept/Caching\u0060 and \u0060pattern/ReadThrough\u0060 to the frontmatter.\n\n### Missing confidence score on [[unscored-topic]]\n\nThe page \u0022Write-Behind Caching\u0022 (file: \u0060unscored-topic.md\u0060) has tags but is missing the required \u0060confidence\u0060 and \u0060confidence_reason\u0060 fields. The page describes a caching pattern with an operational trade-off (acknowledged write loss window). It cites one source implicitly and is internally consistent.\n\n**Proposed remediation**: Add \u0060confidence: medium\u0060 with \u0060confidence_reason: \u0022One source, internally consistent; describes durability trade-off.\u0022\u0060 \u2014 scoring: single source (0), internally consistent pattern description (\u002B1), no contradiction markers (0) = 1 point \u2192 medium threshold.\n\n### Review candidate: [[stale-topic]]\n\nThe page \u0022Sticky Sessions\u0022 (file: \u0060stale-topic.md\u0060) is marked \u0060confidence: low\u0060 with \u0060last_reviewed: 2025-01-05\u0060. This review date is older than the 90-day review window, making it a candidate for re-evaluation. This is an informational note, not an error; a human may wish to review whether the page\u0027s content and confidence assessment remain accurate.\n\n## Structure\n\n### Orphan page: [[orphan-topic]]\n\nThe page \u0022Idempotency Keys\u0022 (file: \u0060orphan-topic.md\u0060) has zero inbound links from any other page or from \u0060index.md\u0060. According to the wiki\u0027s index, this page should be listed in the Technology section alongside related resilience patterns like [[retry-backoff]] and [[circuit-breaker]].\n\n**Proposed remediation**: Add \u0060[[orphan-topic]]\u0060 to the index.md Technology section with an appropriate description. Given the content about idempotency keys for deduplicating retried requests, a description like \u0022Idempotency keys for safely retrying requests\u0022 would fit naturally alongside the retry and circuit-breaker patterns.\n\n\u0060\u0060\u0060proposed-actions\n[\n  {\n    \u0022title\u0022: \u0022Add missing tags to undertagged-topic page\u0022,\n    \u0022description\u0022: \u0022The page undertagged-topic.md (Read-Through Caching) has no tags field. Add tags: [concept/Caching, pattern/ReadThrough] per the Tag Taxonomy.\u0022,\n    \u0022targetPath\u0022: \u0022undertagged-topic.md\u0022\n  },\n  {\n    \u0022title\u0022: \u0022Add missing confidence score to unscored-topic page\u0022,\n    \u0022description\u0022: \u0022The page unscored-topic.md (Write-Behind Caching) has tags but is missing confidence and confidence_reason fields. Add confidence: medium with reason: \u0027One source, internally consistent; describes durability trade-off.\u0027\u0022,\n    \u0022targetPath\u0022: \u0022unscored-topic.md\u0022\n  },\n  {\n    \u0022title\u0022: \u0022Reconcile contradictory cache invalidation strategy claims\u0022,\n    \u0022description\u0022: \u0022Pages cache-invalidation-ttl and cache-invalidation-events make mutually incompatible claims about whether TTL or event-driven invalidation should be the default. Either mark one superseded_by the other, or revise both to clarify when each strategy applies.\u0022,\n    \u0022targetPath\u0022: null\n  },\n  {\n    \u0022title\u0022: \u0022Add orphan-topic to wiki index\u0022,\n    \u0022description\u0022: \u0022The page orphan-topic.md (Idempotency Keys) has zero in